Found while doing k270.

For AMD, OCP::Cmd::Apply::AMDGPU waits until amd.com/gpu shows up in status.allocatable (k251, AMDGPU.pm ~412-443). For NVIDIA, Apply/Workloads.pm (~449-469) and Node/Add.pm _wait_gpu_vendors (~778-794) find NVIDIA nodes by their NFD labels and never wait for nvidia.com/gpu at all. So "GPU stack ready" for NVIDIA means "ClusterPolicy applied", not "a pod can get a GPU".

Done when: after the GPU operator step, apply and node add --gpu wait a bounded time for nvidia.com/gpu allocatable > 0 on every NVIDIA node they act on (never capacity, k106/k270), and report a node that does not get there with its diagnosis on STDERR without failing the join. Follow the AMD readiness pattern. Tests pin allocatable 0 -> warn and allocatable 1 -> ok.

2026-10-31 03:40 Plan (ocp-apply-worker): the wait goes into Workloads::setup_gpu_operator itself, at both returns that are not 'skipped' (unchanged and after a rollout). All three apply callers (Deploy, Drift, after-join) and node add (_ensure_gpu_operator -> _setup_gpu_operator) reach it, so Deploy.pm/Drift.pm/Apply.pm stay untouched. It waits for every NVIDIA node the step detected by NFD label; for node add that includes the added node, which the NFD-label wait has already seen. Waiting on 'unchanged' too is my call: node add runs on a deployed operator nearly every time, and the new node's plugin is exactly what has to come. A node that does not get there is a [WARN] on STDERR, not a failed component. Budget gpu_ready_timeout 300 / gpu_ready_interval 10, the same as AMD. Allocatable via OCP::Kubernetes node_gpu_count/node_gpu_capacity; reads via AMDGPU::read_or_why (k269: only a 404 means not found).

